❓Question:
Given an integer array nums and a target, find the indices of two numbers whose sum equals the target.

For example:
Input:  nums = [2,7,11,15], target = 9
Output: [0,1]
Because nums[0] + nums[1] = 2 + 7 = 9.

✅Approach:
Use a HashMap to store each number and its index. For every number, calculate the value needed to reach the target:
int value = target - nums[i];
Then check if that required value is already in the map. If it is, we have found the two numbers and return their indices. Otherwise, store the current number and its index in the map.

Code:
class Solution {
    public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        HashMap<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            int value = target - nums[i];
            if (map.containsKey(value)) {
                return new int[] {map.get(value), i};
            }
            map.put(nums[i], i);
        }
        return new int[] {};
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)
